'''Salto Bay''' ({{Langx|bl|Baía Salto||leaping bay}}; {{Langx|mao|Aziwàl Sàlto}}; {{Langx|gug|Tahuríka|4=water pool}}) is a shallow {{W|estuary}} in the southeastern portion of the [[Baliscan Levante]], within the [[Alpujarra]], and enclosed by the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]]. It is surrounded by a contiguous region known as [[Concordia]] (and colloquially "Greater Castejón"), and is dominated by the large cities of [[Castejón]], [[Amposta]] and [[Yerba Buena]].

Salto Bay is the {{W|drainage basin}} of the wider [[Alpujarra|Alpujarras]]. Water from the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] mountains, flow into [[Auquinco Bay]], while water from the northern quadrant flows south from [[Limón]], [[El Capitán]] and [[Contra Costa]] into [[Oñoico Bay]]. Water from the [[Alagón (river)|Alagón]] and [[Colorado River|Colorado]] rivers flow into [[Mallín Bay]] which connects at its north end to Salto Bay. It drains to the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] However, this entire group of interconnected bays is often collectively called the ''Salto Bay''. The bay was designated a {{W|Ramsar Convention|Ramsar Wetland of International Importance}} on 2 February 2017.  

